4 System Features

4.1 Dynamic Priority Scheduling

4.1.1 Description and Priority

Implements preemptive scheduling with aging to prevent starvation. Priority: High

• Priority update rule:

– pi(t) = dynamic priority
– α = aging increment

– β = CPU penalty

pi(t + 1) =

(cid:40)

pi(t) − β if CPU-bound
pi(t) + α if waiting

10

4.1.2 Functional Requirements

• REQ-SCH-1: The system shall assign a dynamic priority pi(t) to each process.

• REQ-SCH-2: Waiting processes shall gain priority using aging parameter α.

• REQ-SCH-3: CPU-bound processes shall be penalized using parameter β.

• REQ-SCH-4: The system shall guarantee bounded waiting time.

• REQ-SCH-5: The system shall define excessive CPU usage as CPU execution ex-

ceeding TCP U M AX timer ticks within a scheduling window W .

– Timer Tick: A periodic hardware interrupt generated by the system clock used

as the base unit of time measurement.

– Scheduling Window (W): A logical grouping of timer ticks used to evaluate

process CPU usage behavior for priority adjustment.

• REQ-SCH-6: The default scheduling parameter values shall be:

– αdef ault = 1
– βdef ault = 2
– TCP U M AX def ault = 10 timer ticks
– Wdef ault = 50 timer ticks

• REQ-SCH-7: The allowed configuration ranges shall be:

– 0 ≤ α ≤ 5

– 0 ≤ β ≤ 10

– 1 ≤ TCP U M AX ≤ 100
– 10 ≤ W ≤ 500

4.2 Paging with Copy-On-Write

4.2.1 Description and Priority

Implements memory sharing after fork and duplication upon write. Priority: High

4.2.2 Stimulus/Response Sequences

• fork() called → pages shared

• write to shared page → page fault → copy created

11

4.2.3 Functional Requirements

• REQ-MEM-1: The system shall share pages after fork.

• REQ-MEM-2: The system shall copy a page only upon modification.

• REQ-MEM-3: The system shall maintain reference counting.

• REQ-MEM-4: The system shall handle page faults correctly.

• REQ-MEM-5: The system shall use a fixed page size of 4096 bytes (4KB), consistent

with x86 paging architecture in xv6.

• REQ-MEM-6: Page size shall remain fixed and shall not be configurable in Version

1.0.

• Memory Model:

– N = pages in parent process

– S = page size

– k = modified pages

M emoryCOW = (N + k)S

4.3 File Management System

4.3.1 Description

Responsible for file storage, access, and deletion.

4.3.2 Functional Requirements

• REQ-FILE-1: System shall allow file creation.

• REQ-FILE-2: System shall support reading and writing files.

• REQ-FILE-3: System shall allow file deletion.

• REQ-FILE-4: Files shall be stored in dynamically allocated memory.

4.4 Garbage Collection

4.4.1 Description

Garbage collector reclaims memory from deleted files.

4.4.2 Functional Requirements

• REQ-GC-1: System shall detect unused file blocks.

• REQ-GC-2: Garbage collector shall free unused memory.

• REQ-GC-3: Garbage collection shall run during system idle periods.

12

4.5 Dynamic Memory Compaction

4.5.1 Description

Compacts memory to remove fragmentation.

4.5.2 Functional Requirements

• REQ-DMEM-1: When files are deleted the system shall compact memory.

• REQ-DMEM-2: When the system is idle full memory compaction shall occur.

• REQ-DMEM-3: File references shall be updated after relocation.

5 Other Nonfunctional Requirements

5.1 Performance

• NFR-PERF-1: The scheduler shall guarantee bounded waiting time such that no
runnable process waits more than WM AX timer ticks under normal system load.

• NFR-PERF-2: The Copy-On-Write mechanism shall reduce physical memory du-
plication by at least 30% compared to naive fork implementation under fork-heavy
workloads.

• NFR-PERF-3: Memory compaction shall reduce fragmentation.

5.2 Safety Requirements

• NFR-SAFE-1: No memory corruption shall occur due to concurrent page fault

handling in Copy-On-Write operations.

• NFR-SAFE-2: Invalid memory access shall return an error code and shall not cause

kernel panic.

5.3 Security Requirements

• NFR-SEC-1: No process shall access another process’s memory without explicit

kernel mediation.

5.4 Software Quality Attributes

• NFR-QUAL-1 (Modularity): The system shall maintain architectural separation
between the Scheduler, Memory Manager, and IPC subsystems such that modifi-
cations to one subsystem do not require changes in unrelated subsystems.

• NFR-QUAL-2 (Correctness): All implemented features shall conform to their re-
spective functional requirements (REQ-SCH-*, REQ-MEM-*, REQ-IPC-*), and no
kernel panic shall occur during normal operation under specified workloads.

13

• NFR-QUAL-3 (Extensibility): The system shall allow addition of new scheduling
policies or IPC mechanisms without requiring redesign of the core process abstrac-
tion.

• NFR-QUAL-4 (Reliability): The system shall operate without memory corruption,
deadlock, or kernel crash during continuous execution for at least 10,000 timer ticks
under combined subsystem workload.

A Glossary

• Paging: Fixed size memory management technique.

• Page Size (S): Fixed memory unit of 4096 bytes used for virtual memory paging.

• Copy-On-Write: Delayed memory copying.

• Garbage Collection: Automatic memory reclamation.

• Memory Compaction: Reorganizing memory to reduce fragmentation.

• Aging: Priority improvement over waiting time.

• Starvation: Indefinite waiting.

